Issue #42: Pluggable self-hosted recursive memory with guided Obsidian vault setup

## Product-owner decision
The Green Room hosts only the free harness. It does not host user models, credentials, conversations, rooms, relationship state, or recursive memory.

Users must be able to choose and own their memory backend. A local Obsidian vault is a first-class guided option, alongside a built-in local store and documented self-hosted adapters.

## Required architecture
Define a closed, versioned memory-adapter contract supporting:
- append immutable room events;
- commit bounded episode summaries with source event IDs;
- read/write persona memories;
- read/write directional relationship memories;
- retrieve relevant memories under explicit byte/item/time budgets;
- enumerate, inspect, correct, forget, export, and reset memories;
- health/capability negotiation and schema migration;
- provenance for every derived memory;
- idempotency and conflict behavior.

The harness—not the adapter—owns memory proposal/review policy. Models may propose memory; adapters store only committed records.

## First-class backends
1. **Built-in local** — SQLite/files for normie default; entirely on the user's machine.
2. **Obsidian vault** — Markdown/JSON sidecars in a user-selected vault folder, human-readable and inspectable. Must not require Obsidian to be running. Preserve user notes; write only inside a dedicated configured subtree.
3. **Self-hosted HTTP adapter** — optional localhost/private-network service implementing the versioned contract. No arbitrary command/plugin execution.

Future adapters may include Postgres, filesystem, vector databases, or user-operated services, but must use the same bounded contract.

## Security/privacy requirements
- no project-operated storage or telemetry by default;
- credentials remain local and use OS secret storage/environment indirection;
- no credentials or private memories in room/persona exports unless explicitly selected;
- adapters receive only the minimum scoped record/query, never the entire vault by default;
- path canonicalization, dedicated-root containment, symlink/reparse defense, atomic writes, file locking, crash recovery, and conflict handling;
- self-hosted HTTP defaults to loopback/private transport, explicit origin/endpoint allowlist, bounded timeouts/body sizes, no redirects, and optional user-managed auth;
- adapter response is untrusted data and cannot inject system/runtime instructions;
- memory retrieval remains data, clearly delimited from control instructions;
- event log remains authoritative; derived memories are editable/rebuildable;
- every memory shows why it exists and which events support it.

## Obsidian guided setup
A normie wizard must:
1. explain what is stored and what is not;
2. let the user select/create a vault and Green Room subtree;
3. perform a permission and test-write check;
4. show the exact folders/files Green Room will manage;
5. choose sync responsibility (none, user's Syncthing/iCloud/etc.; Green Room does not configure cloud sync automatically);
6. create a sample room and memory;
7. open/reveal the generated note;
8. test retrieval and deletion;
9. provide backup/export and disconnect instructions.

## Proposed Obsidian subtree
```text
Green Room/
  README.md
  rooms/<room-id>/
    room.md
    events/YYYY-MM.ndjson
    episodes/<episode-id>.md
    people/<persona-id>.md
    relationships/<source>--<target>.md
    memories/<memory-id>.md
    state/index.json
```
Exact on-disk format requires ADR and adversarial review before implementation.

## Acceptance demo
A nontechnical user connects a local Obsidian vault, creates a three-person room, completes ten turns, closes the app, inspects the generated notes in Obsidian, restarts, resumes with relevant episode and relationship memory, corrects one memory, forgets another, exports the room without credentials, and disconnects the vault without data loss.

## Non-goals
- no hosted Green Room memory cloud;
- no arbitrary Python/JS plugin execution;
- no silent whole-vault indexing;
- no automatic Syncthing/iCloud setup;
- no model-written files outside the dedicated managed subtree;
- no treating vector similarity as historical truth.

