Issue #840: [Feature]: Add Bellman-Ford Algorithm Visualizer with Negative Edge Weight Support and Negative Cycle Detection

## Summary

AlgoScope's shortest path section covers Dijkstra's algorithm. The natural, high-value companion to Dijkstra is **Bellman-Ford** — the algorithm that handles negative edge weights (which Dijkstra cannot) and can detect negative weight cycles. Visualizing Bellman-Ford is pedagogically powerful specifically because students who have learned Dijkstra need to understand *why* a different algorithm is needed for negative weights. AlgoScope's existing Comparison Mode makes a Dijkstra vs. Bellman-Ford side-by-side comparison on identical graphs one of the most educationally clear demonstrations possible: showing that Dijkstra gives incorrect results on negative-edge graphs while Bellman-Ford succeeds.

## Why This Adds Unique Value

- Bellman-Ford is consistently tested in technical interviews alongside Dijkstra.
- The question "Why can't Dijkstra handle negative weights?" is best answered visually — showing Dijkstra making a wrong decision on a negative edge, then showing Bellman-Ford correcting it.
- Negative cycle detection is a commonly tested concept with no existing animated demonstration in major visualizers.
- The `(V-1)` relaxation passes are visually compelling — watching every edge relax in rounds until convergence is an animation no other platform offers at this quality level.

## Proposed Implementation

**Step generator (`src/algorithms/searching/bellmanFordEngine.js`):**

```js
export function bellmanFord(nodes, edges, sourceId) {
  const steps = [];
  const V = nodes.length;
  const dist = {};
  const prev = {};

  // Initialize distances
  nodes.forEach(n => { dist[n.id] = Infinity; prev[n.id] = null; });
  dist[sourceId] = 0;
  steps.push({ type: 'INIT', dist: { ...dist }, source: sourceId });

  // Relax edges V-1 times
  for (let pass = 1; pass <= V - 1; pass++) {
    let relaxed = false;
    steps.push({ type: 'START_PASS', pass, totalPasses: V - 1 });

    for (const edge of edges) {
      const { from, to, weight } = edge;
      steps.push({ type: 'EXAMINE_EDGE', from, to, weight, dist: { ...dist } });

      if (dist[from] !== Infinity && dist[from] + weight < dist[to]) {
        const oldDist = dist[to];
        dist[to] = dist[from] + weight;
        prev[to] = from;
        relaxed = true;
        steps.push({
          type: 'RELAX',
          from, to, weight,
          oldDist,
          newDist: dist[to],
          pass,
          description: `Relax (${from}→${to}): ${oldDist === Infinity ? '∞' : oldDist} → ${dist[to]}`
        });
      } else {
        steps.push({ type: 'NO_RELAX', from, to, weight });
      }
    }

    steps.push({ type: 'END_PASS', pass, dist: { ...dist }, anyRelaxed: relaxed });
    if (!relaxed) {
      steps.push({ type: 'EARLY_TERMINATE', pass, reason: 'No relaxations in this pass — already converged.' });
      break;
    }
  }

  // Check for negative cycles (V-th pass)
  steps.push({ type: 'START_NEGATIVE_CYCLE_CHECK' });
  let negativeCycleEdge = null;

  for (const edge of edges) {
    const { from, to, weight } = edge;
    if (dist[from] !== Infinity && dist[from] + weight < dist[to]) {
      negativeCycleEdge = edge;
      steps.push({ type: 'NEGATIVE_CYCLE_FOUND', edge, description: `Negative cycle detected via edge ${from}→${to}` });
      break;
    }
  }

  if (!negativeCycleEdge) {
    steps.push({ type: 'NO_NEGATIVE_CYCLE', dist: { ...dist }, prev: { ...prev } });
  }

  return { dist, prev, negativeCycle: !!negativeCycleEdge, steps };
}
```

