Issue #88: Design: [GenerateInterface] — mirror interfaces from concrete classes (replaces #79)

**TL;DR** — Your design works. I built the target shape by hand before designing the generator and it compiles and behaves: **17/17 checks**, including the nested-generic case you flagged as the hard part. Two calls are yours before I write it.

## Your call (2)

1. **Where do generated interfaces live?** A source generator can only add code to *the assembly it runs in*. So `IStatus` generated from `Status` lands in `SolutionName.DataModels` — **not** `SolutionName.Abstractions`, which today is the dependency root with zero project references. Pick **(A)** move the model classes into `Abstractions` and drop `DataModels`/`DomainModels` as separate projects, or **(B)** model interfaces live beside their class; `Abstractions` keeps service interfaces + exceptions. **Default if you say nothing: B** — smaller, deletes no projects.
2. **Mirror the generic bases you hand-write, or infer them?** Mirroring copies type parameters and constraints verbatim, one pass. Inferring means working out which members are custom types and threading parameters up transitively — the "iterative generation" you named. **Default: mirror in v1, infer in v2**, because mirroring is what I have proof for.

## Answering your one question

**Yes, the generic base can be abstract** — you asked me to confirm. Verified on both the class and the interface. The route-facing contract stays concrete, which is all `System.Text.Json` needs.

<details>
<summary><b>The shape, proven — this is what the generator has to emit</b></summary>

Three levels, `Order` → `Customer` → `Address`, exactly as you described it. A leaf needs no generic base; a type with custom-typed members becomes generic in them.

```csharp
// LEAF — nothing varies between layers, so no generic base.
public interface IAddress { string Line1 { get; set; } }
public class Address : IAddress { public string Line1 { get; set; } = ""; }
public class DomainAddress : Address, IDomainAddress { public string Normalised() => Line1.ToUpperInvariant(); }

// HAS A CUSTOM-TYPED MEMBER — generic in it, abstract, constrained by the mirror interface.
public interface ICustomerBase<TAddress> where TAddress : IAddress
{
    string Name { get; set; }
    TAddress Address { get; set; }
}
public abstract class CustomerBase<TAddress> : ICustomerBase<TAddress> where TAddress : IAddress
{
    public string Name { get; set; } = "";
    public TAddress Address { get; set; } = default!;
}

// The contract used in the route — closes the generic over CONCRETE types.
public interface ICustomer : ICustomerBase<Address> { }
public class Customer : CustomerBase<Address>, ICustomer { }

// The domain model — same base, domain types.
public interface IDomainCustomer : ICustomerBase<DomainAddress> { }
public class DomainCustomer : CustomerBase<DomainAddress>, IDomainCustomer { }
```

**Nesting a type that is itself generic** is where the parameters multiply. `Order` holds a `Customer`, and `Customer`'s constraint mentions `TAddress`, so `TAddress` has to be threaded through `Order` too:

```csharp
public interface IOrderBase<TCustomer, TAddress>
    where TCustomer : ICustomerBase<TAddress>
    where TAddress : IAddress
{
    string Id { get; set; }
    TCustomer Customer { get; set; }
}

public interface IOrder : IOrderBase<Customer, Address> { }
public class Order : OrderBase<Customer, Address>, IOrder { }

public interface IDomainOrder : IOrderBase<DomainCustomer, DomainAddress> { string Describe(); }
public class DomainOrder : OrderBase<DomainCustomer, DomainAddress>, IDomainOrder { ... }
```

**The payoff, and the reason it's worth the machinery:** one generic method now accepts both layers, and DI/mocking work against the interface.

```csharp
static string IdOf<TOrder, TCustomer, TAddress>(TOrder o)
    where TOrder : IOrderBase<TCustomer, TAddress>
    where TCustomer : ICustomerBase<TAddress>
    where TAddress : IAddress
    => o.Id;
```

</details>

<details>
