Issue #403: Render item activity as an attributable response thread

Parent: #271

Related: #273, #297, #307, #276, #399, #149

## Observation

The hosted dashboard can show an item's bounded history, runs, artifacts, dependencies, and current actors, but the detail view still reads like a flat event log. During dogfood, several ChatGPT workers publish through one GitHub identity, reviewers respond on later commits, and work may continue across chats or runner surfaces. The human needs to see who responded to whom, which run produced each update, and where the live or historical external surface can be opened.

A visually nested thread must never invent causal relationships from timestamps, adjacent rows, matching callsigns, or GitHub ordering. Explicit event, run, reply, continuation, provider, or causal references remain the only basis for nesting.

## Goal

Turn the read-only item activity area into a compact, attributable response thread that helps the human answer:

- who or what produced this update;
- which run, generation, and callsign it belongs to;
- whether it responds to an earlier event, review, decision, handoff, or continuation;
- what changed and which evidence/artifact/head supports it;
- whether the related ChatGPT/Codex/runner surface can be opened safely;
- which project and active worker the entry belongs to;
- whether the history window is complete or explicitly partial.

## Thread model

Each rendered entry should bind only bounded public fields already authorised for the item view:

- exact event ID and timestamp;
- event class and concise public summary;
- actor ID, display name, kind, and current or historical callsign when available;
- run ID and exact run generation when available;
- claim/authority generation when relevant to the event;
- explicit parent/reply/causation reference when present;
- referenced artifact, commit, PR, review, decision, continuation, or handoff;
- public state such as progress, review requested, accepted, repair requested, blocked, resumed, completed, or superseded;
- history completeness/truncation state.

Unknown or missing relationship data stays chronological and unnested. One entry may display a bounded “related to” link without claiming a direct reply.

## External surface links

Issue #273 owns private ChatGPT/Codex/runner attachments. Once its read projection exists, the thread may show:

- surface kind and Stensibly-owned label;
- current/stale/detached/inaccessible state;
- authorised open-in-surface action;
- evidence-only versus human-resumable versus adapter-resumable classification;
- fresh-session continuation action when the old surface is unavailable.

Private deep links and provider references remain absent from public feeds, unauthorised REST/MCP results, copied summaries, and static HTML. A chat URL never grants authority, responsibility, approval, or identity continuity.

## Project and actor navigation

- keep the current item and project boundary explicit;
- allow bounded filtering by actor/callsign, run, event class, and evidence type;
- show separate workers even when they share one GitHub account;
- preserve the canonical project filter from the dashboard;
- provide direct navigation to item, run, artifact, and authorised external-surface detail;
- retain a useful chronological view when filters are cleared.

## First implementation slice

Use existing bounded hosted item-detail projections only:

1. replace the flat activity list with accessible thread-style entries;
2. display actor/callsign and run identity when those fields already exist;
3. group only explicit relationships; leave other events chronological;
4. surface `eventsTruncated` prominently and avoid “complete history” language;
5. link existing safe artifact/commit/PR references;
6. add actor/run/event filters without changing server state;
7. reserve a typed UI slot for #273 attachments, rendering nothing when the authorised projection is absent.

No reply composer, provider mutation, chat export, undocumented ChatGPT API, external-session enumeration, automatic sharing, or inferred continuation belongs in this slice.

## Follow-up data contract

Where current public events cannot express replies or causal grouping, add a separate reviewed server slice under #149. It should carry explicit bounded relationship references and sequence data; the UI must not reverse-engineer them from prose.

## Tests

Cover at least:

- two callsigns under one GitHub identity remain distinct;
- explicit parent/causation links nest correctly;
- unlinked events stay chronological and unnested;
- stale/superseded runs are labelled accurately;
- private execution-envelope/result rows remain absent;
- hidden cross-project dependencies remain absent;
- `eventsTruncated` produces a visible partial-history notice;
- actor/run/event filters are bounded and resettable;
- keyboard navigation, focus order, narrow-phone layout, and reduced motion;
- missing/inaccessible external surfaces preserve a useful thread;
- private surface links appear only for an authorised hosted session;
- dashboard, REST, and MCP continue to agree on public event identity and completeness.

## Acceptance

- the human can follow item activity as a legible conversation among distinct people and agents;
- every nested relationship is backed by an explicit durable reference;
- shared GitHub identity does not collapse worker attribution;
- project boundaries and private execution data remain intact;
- external chat links improve navigation while Stensibly remains canonical and resumable without them;
- partial history is visible and cannot be mistaken for a complete thread.

