Issue #352: [Performance] Nested roles_obj mapping in OML reduces throughput to approximately 70k EPS

# [Performance] Nested `roles_obj` mapping in OML reduces throughput to approximately 70k EPS

Report type: Performance issue  
Repository: `wp-labs/warp-parse`

## Description

After adding the following OML definition to construct `roles_obj`, the throughput of the current test pipeline was observed at approximately **70,000 EPS (70k EPS)**. The main performance bottleneck has not yet been confirmed.

The suspected cost areas are:

- construction of deeply nested objects;
- repeated field reads and object assembly under `source` and `target`;
- repeated reads of the same input fields, such as `__sip`, `__dip`, and `devIp`;
- creation of the `ip_addresses` array object.

These are initial hypotheses only. No profiler result or minimal controlled comparison is available yet.

## Impact

This OML definition reduces processing capacity in the current alert-parsing scenario. If the input rate approaches or exceeds the observed throughput, it may cause backlog growth, increased latency, or higher resource utilization.

## OML definition

```oml
roles_obj = object {
  carriers = array {};
  related = array {};
  source = object {
    endpoint = object {
      ip = read(__sip);
      ipv4 = read(__sip);
      port = read(__sport);
    };
    geo = object {
      continent = object { name = read(__sip_continent_name); };
      country = object { code = read(__sip_country_code); name = read(__sip_country_name); };
      region = object { name = read(__sip_province_name); };
      city = object { name = read(__sip_city_name); };
      coordinates = object { latitude = read(__sip_latitude); longitude = read(__sip_longitude); };
    };
    resource = object {
      id = read(__sip_asset_id);
      name = read(__sip_asset_name);
      type = read(__sip_asset_type);
      system = object { id = read(__sip_system_id); name = read(__sip_system_name); };
      organization = object { name = read(__sip_company_name); };
    };
  };
  target = object {
    endpoint = object {
      ip = read(__dip);
      ipv4 = read(__dip);
      port = read(__dport);
    };
    domain = object { name = read(__target_domain); };
    geo = object {
      continent = object { name = read(__dip_continent_name); };
      country = object { code = read(__dip_country_code); name = read(__dip_country_name); };
      region = object { name = read(__dip_province_name); };
      city = object { name = read(__dip_city_name); };
      coordinates = object { latitude = read(__dip_latitude); longitude = read(__dip_longitude); };
    };
    resource = object {
      id = read(__dip_asset_id);
      name = read(__dip_asset_name);
      type = read(__dip_asset_type);
      system = object { id = read(__dip_system_id); name = read(__dip_system_name); };
      organization = object { name = read(__dip_company_name); };
    };
  };
  observer = object {
    product = object { name = chars(ngsoc); };
    device = object {
      vendor = chars(qax);
      ip = read(devIp);
      ipv4 = read(devIp);
      ip_addresses = array {
        object { address = read(devIp); };
      };
    };
  };
};
```

## Expected result

Please help confirm:

1. Whether the regression is caused by nested object construction, repeated field reads, or array construction.
2. Whether the OML executor can optimize repeated reads, constant `chars` values, or empty arrays.
3. Whether there is an equivalent recommended OML form with lower runtime overhead.
4. If this is a known implementation limitation, please document the relevant performance boundary or usage guidance.

## Related issue

- [#346 — OML support for nested objects and object arrays](https://github.com/wp-labs/warp-parse/issues/346)



